# The pipeline — plan, implement, verify

Every issue that will change this repository runs through three phases, in this order, whoever
started the run: Linear delegation, Slack, the web dashboard, the IDE, the CLI. There is no separate
path for "small" issues; there is an exemption, recorded on the record, for issues that are not
changes at all.

| phase | model | who runs it | may edit files |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. plan | Opus (`claude-opus-5`) | `planner` subagent | no — read-only |
| 2. implement | Sonnet (`claude-sonnet-5`) | `implementer` subagent | yes |
| 3. verify | Opus (`claude-opus-5`) | `verifier` subagent | no — read-only |

The models are pinned in `.cursor/agents/{planner,implementer,verifier}.md`. Those files are the
single source of truth for which model owns which phase; do not restate a model id anywhere else.

## How a run goes

1. **Read the ledger first.** `python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py digest`. This is what every previous
   run learned. Read it before you plan, not after you are stuck.
2. **Plan.** Delegate to the `planner` subagent with the issue id, the issue text and this session's
   conversation id. Wait for the plan. Do not edit a file before a plan exists — not even an obvious
   one-line fix, because the plan is what the verifier checks against.
3. **Implement.** Delegate to the `implementer` subagent, passing the plan verbatim and the
   conversation id. Sonnet implements a settled plan; it does not re-open the design.
4. **Verify.** Delegate to the `verifier` subagent with the plan and the diff. On `fail`, hand the
   findings back to the `implementer` and verify again. A run may loop 2 and 3; it may not skip 4.
5. **Report** the verifier's verdict, with the proof command's captured output.

If you are already running on Opus you may perform the plan phase yourself instead of delegating —
the requirement is that the phase happens on Opus and is recorded, not that a subagent exists. The
implement phase always delegates: it is the only way this repository gets Sonnet to do it.

## The ledger is the recursion

`docs/agent-ledger/ledger.jsonl` — append-only, one JSON object per line, hash-chained per session.
It is the only thing that survives between runs, so it is where learning has to live.

- Every phase appends exactly one entry before it hands over, naming **the model that actually ran
  it** and **one lesson** a future run would otherwise have to rediscover.
- Never edit or delete a line. `validate` recomputes the chain and a rewritten entry shows up as a
  broken hash. Same discipline as bronze, for the same reason: the record is worth less than nothing
  if it can be tidied.
- A lesson is a constraint, a trap, or a disproved assumption — phrased as an instruction to the
  next run. "Be careful with dbt" is not a lesson. "`dbt test` needs `dbt deps` first; a clean clone
  has no packages directory" is.
- A lesson that recurs three times has earned a rule: `python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py promote`
  lists candidates, and `CLAUDE.md` takes them as a dated diff naming the runs behind it. That is
  the loop closing — the ledger teaches the rules file, which is read on every future turn.

```bash
python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py digest                 # what earlier runs learned
python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py append --phase plan …   # leave your entry
python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py check --session <id>    # what this run still owes
python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py validate                # nothing was rewritten
python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py promote                 # lessons that have earned a rule
./scripts/prove_agent_pipeline.sh                       # the pipeline proves itself
```

## What is enforced, not merely asked for

`.cursor/hooks.json` runs `.cursor/hooks/pipeline_hook.py` on four events:

- **`preToolUse`** — briefs the run once with this protocol and the ledger digest, and appends the
  digest to every `Task` prompt, so a delegated subagent receives the accumulated lessons even if
  the parent forgets to pass them.
- **`subagentStart`** — records which model actually ran each phase. If the pinned model did not
  take effect, the mismatch goes into the ledger as a `note`; the phase is not silently mislabelled.
- **`subagentStop`** — reminds the parent to append the finished phase's entry, and names the next
  phase.
- **`stop`** — a run that changed the repository cannot finish while any of the three entries is
  missing, or while the ledger fails validation.

Hooks fail open: a bug there degrades this to a convention rather than wedging the session. That is
why the protocol is written here too.

## The exemption

A run that changes nothing — a question, a code reading, an investigation — is not an issue and does
not need three models. The `stop` hook only asks when the run has a diff or new commits. When the
pipeline genuinely does not apply, close it on the record:

```bash
python3 scripts/agent_ledger.py append --phase exempt --model <the model you are> \
  --session <conversation id> --summary "<why the pipeline did not apply>" --allow-no-lesson
```

Claiming an exemption for work that changed the repository is the one thing this protocol treats as
dishonest rather than merely wrong.
